WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

NOTE
Module WB-M01 "Why Processes Fail People". Cue times are authored estimates at 140 wpm and
are replaced with measured times after the recording session. Cue IDs are stable and are
referenced by the build; re-timing changes times only, never IDs or text.

NOTE Screen 1 — Hook

s1-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.500
Think about the last time your workday went sideways.

s1-2
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:14.000
Maybe it was information you needed and couldn't find. A system that made you
type the same thing twice.

s1-3
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:20.500
An approval that sat in somebody's inbox for three days while a customer waited.

s1-4
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:28.000
Now hold that moment in your mind, because we're going to ask an uncomfortable
question about it.

s1-5
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.000
Was that a lazy person problem — or a badly designed process problem?

s1-6
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:40.500
Most of us are trained to answer that question with a name. Someone dropped the
ball. Someone didn't check.

s1-7
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:48.000
Over the next few minutes I want to show you why that answer is usually wrong,
and why the alternative is far more useful to you.

NOTE Screen 2 — The 94/6 rule of thumb

s2-1
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.500
This is the finding that started modern quality thinking.

s2-2
00:00:53.500 --> 00:01:03.000
W. Edwards Deming, working with Japanese manufacturers after the second world war,
put a number on it:

s2-3
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:12.500
about ninety-four percent of performance problems come from the system — the way
the work is designed and managed.

s2-4
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:21.000
Only about six percent come from what he called special causes: one person, one
machine, one unusual day.

s2-5
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:30.500
Two honesty notes before we go on. The exact numbers are a rule of thumb, not a
measurement; Joseph Juran had earlier put it at eighty-five and fifteen.

s2-6
00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:38.000
And Deming was making a technical point about two kinds of variation, which you'll
meet properly at Yellow Belt.

s2-7
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:47.000
But the direction is what matters, and on the direction the evidence has been
remarkably consistent for seventy years.

s2-8
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:55.500
When something goes wrong at work, the way the work is set up is the overwhelming
favourite. The person is the long shot.

NOTE Screen 3 — The mindset shift

s3-1
00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:04.000
So here is the shift that every improvement method is built on, and it fits on one
line: blame the process, not the person.

s3-2
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Then fix the process.

s3-3
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:14.000
I want to be careful here, because this is often misheard in two directions.

s3-4
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:21.000
It does not mean nobody is ever accountable for anything. And it does not mean
people are helpless.

s3-5
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:30.500
What it means is narrower and more practical. When you are staring at a problem
that keeps happening, the most productive first question is not who did this.

s3-6
00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:37.000
It is: what about the way this work is set up made that likely?

s3-7
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:44.000
That question is worth learning to ask out loud, because it changes what a room
does next.

NOTE Screen 4 — Reason one: it's more accurate

s4-1
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:51.000
There are two reasons to work this way, and the first one is simply that it is
more accurate.

s4-2
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:58.500
Think about what happens when a recurring problem gets treated as a people
problem. The person is coached, or moved, or replaced.

s4-3
00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:08.000
And then the next person arrives — conscientious, well-intentioned, new — and
inherits the same layout, the same forms,

s4-4
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:15.500
the same two systems that don't talk to each other. Within a few weeks, the same
thing goes wrong again.

s4-5
00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:22.000
That pattern is the single most reliable signal you will learn to spot.

s4-6
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:30.500
If a problem survives a change of personnel, the problem was never in the
personnel.

s4-7
00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:37.000
It is built into the work, waiting for whoever shows up next.

NOTE Screen 5 — Reason two: it's safer to talk about

s5-1
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:44.000
The second reason matters even more, and it has nothing to do with being nice.

s5-2
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Think about what you do when you make a mistake somewhere that treats mistakes as
personal failures. You fix it quietly. You work around it.

s5-3
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:01.000
You do not raise it in a meeting. That is not a character flaw — it is a
completely rational response to the situation.

s5-4
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Now think about what that means for the organisation. Every hidden problem is a
problem nobody can fix,

s5-5
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:14.500
and it keeps costing money and time and goodwill in the dark.

s5-6
00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:24.000
This is why the language matters. When problems are framed as flaws in how work is
designed, people will tell you about them —

s5-7
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.500
because there is nothing personal to defend.

s5-8
00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:35.000
A workplace that hides its problems cannot improve. It's that simple.

NOTE Screen 6 — Three workplaces, one story

s6-1
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:42.500
Let's make this concrete, because the pattern looks identical in wildly different
places.

s6-2
00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:50.000
On a packing line, someone keeps shipping the wrong variant of a product.

s6-3
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:58.500
Look at the station: two nearly identical bins, side by side, with small labels.
A new packer will make that same error inside a week.

s6-4
00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:05.000
On a hospital ward, a nurse "forgets" a discharge step.

s6-5
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:14.500
Look at the work: the checklist lives in one system and the nurse charts in
another. Nothing about that arrangement helps anyone remember anything.

s6-6
00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:21.000
In an office, an analyst sends out figures that turn out to be stale.

s6-7
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:30.500
Look at the folder: there are three files with "final" in the name and nothing at
all marking which one is current.

s6-8
00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:38.000
In every one of those cases, replacing the person changes nothing.

s6-9
00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:47.000
Changing the bins, the systems, the file naming — that changes everything. Same
story, three industries.

s6-10
00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:53.500
You will start seeing it everywhere, and that is the point.

NOTE Screen 7 — Your turn

s7-1
00:05:53.500 --> 00:06:01.000
Now it's your turn, and this is the part of the module that does the real work.

s7-2
00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:08.500
Think of one thing that goes wrong repeatedly in your own job. Write it down twice.

s7-3
00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:15.000
First as a person problem — the way it probably gets discussed today.

s7-4
00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:22.000
Then as a process problem — what about the setup makes it likely?

s7-5
00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:30.500
Then read both back and ask yourself which version hands you something you could
actually change.

s7-6
00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:37.000
There is no scoring here and nobody is grading your writing. Take the time.

NOTE Screen 8 — Takeaways

s8-1
00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:42.000
Three things to carry out of this module.

s8-2
00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:50.500
First: most recurring problems are built into the process, so changing people
rarely fixes them.

s8-3
00:06:50.500 --> 00:07:00.000
Second: framing problems as process flaws is what makes them safe to surface — and
a surfaced problem is the raw material of every improvement you will ever make.

s8-4
00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:08.500
Third, and this is the one I'd ask you to hold onto: your frustrations are data.

s8-5
00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:15.000
This course is going to teach you how to read them.

s8-6
00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:20.000
Next up: what a process actually is.
